173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
184:- **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
185:- <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
230:- **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
231:- **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
236:- **Q6.1** What determines mandatory static refusal? — Fixed Lean-defined local refusal rules. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): static refusal widens from proven violations to unproven safety.*
237:- **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
252:- **Q7.10** Do you approve the linux-x86_64-hosted grant table? — Approve R4 F6's complete grant table below as part of this row: every launch configuration, operation signature, permission, narrowing rule and ending contract. Standard-stream operations require Console (Q7.11), not Device; where the R4 F6 table says with(Device) for StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, read with(Console). A missing configured grant refuses startup with exit 125; a trap exits 134. *Revises or adds: New target contract: owner decision.* *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.*
258:- **Q8.5** What launcher ABI and observations are fixed? — Pinned launcher ABI, inputs, startup and trap mapping. *Extended 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the stack target contract (adequate stack excludes exhaustion, launch refusal 125 when the reservation is smaller than the bound; then a defined outcome for inadequate stack) is a future target-contract obligation, not a current guarantee.*
271:- **Q9.11** What do contracts mean and when are they checked? — Erased logical contracts; ensures sees result and surviving parameters after cleanup; no snapshot construct. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.*
290:- **P8** Server services under scripts/ — Keep only landing services: train, review, hook, landing bot. The status page, work queue, fuzz farm, perf tracking and nightly campaign all stop and are recorded as archived in RETENTION.tsv; each returns only with a roadmap task.
296:- **P14** ci-3 handover: an exception to the archive requirement — Done by the owner on 2026-11-04: concrete-ci-3 was handed to stabileo; its fuzz corpus, perf history and leanprof data were not copied (an exception to the archive requirement).
304:- **P21** Retention records outlive legacy/ — RETENTION.tsv, ISSUES.tsv, required-checks.txt, scripts/oracle/ and docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/ (with task_disposition.tsv) are part of the day-one root, outside legacy/, so they survive deleting it. *Revised 2026-11-06 (owner, verbatim: "do the whole movement"): of docs/archive/roadmap-2026-11/, the files that are decision sources or disposition records stay at the root (task_disposition.tsv, the decision log ROADMAP_DECISIONS.md, the task register ROADMAP_TASKS.md, ROADMAP_RECONCILIATION.md, ROADMAP_RESTRUCTURE.md with its table, and the archive README); the old control plane ROADMAP.md, ROADMAP_BACKLOG.md, ROADMAP_DIRECTION.md and WORK_QUEUE.md were duplicates of their frozen legacy/ copies apart from the move's mechanical rewrites, and nothing cites them as a decision source, so their root copies were removed and links point at the legacy/ copies, which stay until the legacy deletion point. The other docs/archive/ records (the site takedown record, the old bug ledger's disposition, the landing audit and the dated decision archives) stay at the root.*
306:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
